### 9\. Important correction to the timing pattern

For all these implementations, follow this structure:

```java
long startTime = ApiResponseTimeUtil.startTime();

ResponseDTO response;

try {

    response = sendFlexWebClientService
            .sendFlexRequest(...)
            .block();

} catch (Exception e) {

    long responseTime =
            ApiResponseTimeUtil.calculateResponseTimeInMillis(startTime);

    // Persist failure with responseTime

    throw e;
}

long responseTime =
        ApiResponseTimeUtil.calculateResponseTimeInMillis(startTime);

// Persist success with responseTime

return response;
```

Why do I recommend this structure instead of putting the persistence call inside the `try` block?

Suppose the SendFlex API succeeds, but saving its response to the database fails.

If the persistence call is inside the same `try`, the catch block can mistakenly treat the database exception as a SendFlex API failure and attempt another persistence operation.

Separating the API execution from database persistence avoids that problem.

Also, do not include DTO mapping, database persistence or other business processing in the measured duration. Your objective is to measure the SendFlex API call itself.

